\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Normal ordering adds a matrix-trace central character}

\p{Let #{K} be a field in which #{2} is invertible. On
#{V=M_N(K)} take

##{Q_{\mathrm{tr}}(Y)=\operatorname{Tr}(Y^2),\qquad
B_{Q_{\mathrm{tr}}}(X,Y)=2\operatorname{Tr}(XY),}

and write #{d_{ij}=\iota_{Q_{\mathrm{tr}}}(E_{ij})}. The Clifford
relation is

##{d_{ij}d_{kl}+d_{kl}d_{ij}
=2\delta_{jk}\delta_{li}.}

Let #{q(X)} be the quadratic Clifford lift of the adjoint action
#{Y\mapsto[X,Y]}. The normal-ordered lift used for the CAR module is

##{D(X)=q(X)+\frac N2\operatorname{Tr}(X)1.}

On a matrix unit it has the concrete form

##{D(E_{ij})=\frac12\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}d_{ik}d_{kj}.}

Indeed,
#{d_{ik}d_{kj}=\beta_{Q_{\mathrm{tr}}}(E_{ik},E_{kj})
+\delta_{ij}1}; summing the contraction term over #{k} gives
#{(N/2)\delta_{ij}1}. This derives both the coefficient and its positive
sign.}

\p{There are three different actions here. The ordinary exterior
extension of the adjoint action is the derivation

##{\delta_X(Y_1\wedge\cdots\wedge Y_r)
=\sum_sY_1\wedge\cdots\wedge[X,Y_s]\wedge\cdots\wedge Y_r.}

The quadratic Clifford action is the inner derivation
#{[q(X),-]=[D(X),-]}. The CAR representation on the full Clifford
algebra is instead left multiplication,

##{\rho_{\mathrm{CAR}}(X)c=D(X)c,
\qquad \rho_{\mathrm{CAR}}(X)c-cD(X)=[D(X),c].}

On the central matrix the three formulas read:

##{\operatorname{ad}_I=0,\qquad q(I)=0,
\qquad D(I)=\frac{N^2}{2}1.}

The left-regular carrier has dimension #{2^{N^2}}. For #{N\geq 1} it
is not an irreducible exterior spinor carrier; #{N=0} is the
one-dimensional trivial exception.}

\p{The exact identities are formalized at the pinned TauCeti revision.
The
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/GeneralLinear/Clifford.lean#L90-L98}{normal-ordered lift},
its
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/GeneralLinear/Clifford.lean#L198-L268}{matrix-unit and generator formulas},
and the
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/GeneralLinear/Fock.lean#L99-L134}{left-action comparison}
have the field and inverse-two hypotheses stated above. They do not
identify this module with an irreducible spinor representation.}

\p{\citet{Section 12.1, pp. 230--233}{pressley1986loop}
let the determinant-square-root cover of #{U(W)} act on
#{\bigwedge^kW} by #{u^{-1}\bigwedge^k(A)}, with #{u^2=\det A}.
Differentiating first along a unitary curve gives
#{d\bigwedge(X)-\frac12\operatorname{Tr}_W(X)} for
#{X\in\mathfrak u(W)}; complexifying then gives the corresponding
#{\mathfrak{gl}(W)} formula. No arbitrary complex matrix is being
presented as the derivative of a unitary curve.}

\p{\citet{Section 2, Example 2.5(3), preprint pp. 8--9}{panyushev2001exterior}
works over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and gives
the ordinary spinor correction as
#{\mathbb k_{-\nu}\otimes\bigwedge W}, where #{\nu} is half the sum of
the weights with multiplicity. Evaluating #{\nu} is half the exterior
trace. These two sources agree on
the ordinary minus-half-trace normalization. They do not produce the
positive #{(N/2)\operatorname{Tr}} term above: the trace of
#{\operatorname{ad}_X} on #{M_N} is zero, while that term is the
contraction created by normal ordering on the full left-regular
Clifford module.}
